    entry .apply   `S8(bits: B8)`: reads a bit pattern as a signed integer
    entry .inquality   signed comparison for `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `min` and `max`
        The `S8 is Orderable` given, in implicit scope through the companion; it is the
        counterpart of the `orderable` given of every other numeric type, under a different
        name.
  entry .F64   a 64-bit IEEE floating-point number, with mixed arithmetic against anything that widens to it; see `hypotenuse.F64`
    detail
        The companion of the opaque type `F64`, exported with it to the `hypotenuse` package.
    entry .Min   `Double.MinValue`, the most negative finite value
    entry .Max   `Double.MaxValue`
    entry .underlying   evidence that an `F64` is a `Double` underneath
    entry .Addition   the `F64 + F64` instance, a singleton object so that `add` inlines to `dadd`
    entry .Subtraction   the `F64 - F64` instance, a singleton object
    entry .Multiplication   the `F64 * F64` instance, a singleton object
    entry .Division   the `F64 / F64` instance, a singleton object
    entry .Negation   the prefix `-` instance for `F64`, a singleton object
    entry .addable   `F64 + F64`, the `Addition` object by its singleton type
        In implicit scope through the companion, so a generic `[n: Addable by n to n]` bound is
        satisfied by `F64` with no import.
    entry .subtractable   `F64 - F64`, the `Subtraction` object by its singleton type
    entry .multiplicable   `F64 * F64`, the `Multiplication` object by its singleton type
    entry .divisible   `F64 / F64`, the `Division` object by its singleton type
    entry .negatable   prefix `-` on an `F64`, the `Negation` object by its singleton type
    entry .Widening   a conversion into `F64` which also carries the arithmetic of an `F64` with that operand
      detail
          `Widening[right]` extends `Conversion[right, F64]` and holds four singleton objects,
          `Addition`, `Subtraction`, `Multiplication` and `Division`, each an instance of the
          corresponding symbolism typeclass for an `F64` on the left and a `right` on the
          right: the right operand is converted and the operation is the primitive one. Every
          conversion object in this companion (`DoubleConversion`, `IntConversion`,
          `U8Conversion`, …) extends it, and the `addableWidening` family of givens finds the
          instances through it, which is how `F64(1.5)*2` and `F64(0.5) + u8` resolve. A
          library adding a new operand type for `F64` arithmetic gives an object extending
          `Widening[T]` and a given of its singleton type.
      entry #Addition   `F64 + right`, converting the right operand first
      entry #Subtraction   `F64 - right`
      entry #Multiplication   `F64 * right`
      entry #Division   `F64 / right`
    entry .addableWidening   `F64 + right` for any `right` with a `Widening`, as `F64 is Addable by right to F64`
        Transparent, with a plain refinement type rather than a dependent one, so that the
        search fixes `right` from the expected operand before it looks for the `Widening`; the
        summoned instance still has the object's singleton type, through which `add` inlines.
    entry .subtractableWidening   `F64 - right` for any `right` with a `Widening`
    entry .multiplicableWidening   `F64 * right` for any `right` with a `Widening`
    entry .divisibleWidening   `F64 / right` for any `right` with a `Widening`
    entry .canEqual   an `F64` may be compared with `==` to `F64`, the signed integers, `Double`, `Long`, `Int`, `Short` and `Byte`
    entry .apply   `F64(double)`, `F64(bits: B64)`, or `F64(sign, exponent: B16, mantissa: B64)`
        `F64(bits)` is `Double.longBitsToDouble`; the three-argument form assembles the IEEE
        pattern from its sign bit, biased exponent and 52-bit mantissa.
    entry .orderable   comparison of two `F64`s for `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `min` and `max`
        IEEE comparison, so every comparison with `NaN` is false.
    entry .commensurableInt   `f64 < 2`: comparison of an `F64` against an `Int`
    entry .commensurable   `f64 < 2.5`: comparison of an `F64` against a `Double`
    entry .DoubleConversion   the `Widening[Double]`: a `Double` is already an `F64`
    entry .doubleConversion   the given which lets a `Double` stand where an `F64` is wanted, and be an operand of one
    entry .FloatConversion   the `Widening[Float]`
    entry .floatConversion   the given converting a `Float` to `F64`
    entry .IntConversion   the `Widening[Int]`
    entry .intConversion   the given converting an `Int` to `F64`, exactly
    entry .ShortConversion   the `Widening[Short]`
    entry .shortConversion   the given converting a `Short` to `F64`
    entry .ByteConversion   the `Widening[Byte]`
    entry .byteConversion   the given converting a `Byte` to `F64`
    entry .U32Conversion   the `Widening[U32]`, by the unsigned value
    entry .u32Conversion   the given converting a `U32` to `F64`, exactly
    entry .S32Conversion   the `Widening[S32]`
    entry .s32Conversion   the given converting an `S32` to `F64`, exactly
    entry .U16Conversion   the `Widening[U16]`, by the unsigned value
    entry .u16Conversion   the given converting a `U16` to `F64`
    entry .U8Conversion   the `Widening[U8]`, by the unsigned value, so `U8(200)` is `200.0`
    entry .u8Conversion   the given converting a `U8` to `F64`
    entry .S16Conversion   the `Widening[S16]`
    entry .s16Conversion   the given converting an `S16` to `F64`
    entry .S8Conversion   the `Widening[S8]`
    entry .s8Conversion   the given converting an `S8` to `F64`
