  entry .B64   a 64-bit pattern: shifts, rotations, masks and bit addressing, but no arithmetic; see `hypotenuse.B64`
    detail
        The companion of the opaque type `B64`, exported with it to the `hypotenuse` package.
    entry .underlying   evidence that a `B64` is a `Long` underneath
    entry .block   `B64.block(n)`: the lowest `n` bits set
    entry .set   a mask with one bit set, by ordinal, or a run of bits set, by interval
        `B64.set(Ter)` is `0b100`; `B64.set(Prim thru Ter)` is `0b111`.
    entry .apply   a `B64` from a `Long`, `Int`, `Short` or `Byte`, or from eight big-endian bytes
        `B64(bytes, offset = 0)` reads `bytes(offset)` as the most significant byte. The
        narrower primitives are sign-extended, as `toLong` does.
  entry .B32   a 32-bit pattern: shifts, rotations, masks and bit addressing, but no arithmetic; see `hypotenuse.B32`
    detail
        The companion of the opaque type `B32`, exported with it to the `hypotenuse` package.
    entry .underlying   evidence that a `B32` is an `Int` underneath
    entry .block   `B32.block(n)`: the lowest `n` bits set
    entry .set   a mask with one bit set, by ordinal, or a run of bits set, by interval
    entry .apply   a `B32` from an `Int`, `Short` or `Byte`, or from four big-endian bytes at an offset
  entry .B16   a 16-bit pattern: shifts, rotations, masks and bit addressing, but no arithmetic; see `hypotenuse.B16`
    detail
        The companion of the opaque type `B16`, exported with it to the `hypotenuse` package.
    entry .underlying   evidence that a `B16` is a `Short` underneath
    entry .block   `B16.block(n)`: the lowest `n` bits set
    entry .set   a mask with one bit set, by ordinal, or a run of bits set, by interval
    entry .apply   a `B16` from a `Short` or `Byte`, or from two big-endian bytes at an offset
  entry .B8   an 8-bit pattern: shifts, rotations, masks and bit addressing, but no arithmetic; see `hypotenuse.B8`
    detail
        The companion of the opaque type `B8`, exported with it to the `hypotenuse` package.
    entry .underlying   evidence that a `B8` is a `Byte` underneath
    entry .block   `B8.block(n)`: the lowest `n` bits set
    entry .set   a mask with one bit set, by ordinal, or a run of bits set, by interval
    entry .apply   `B8(byte)`: a pattern from a `Byte`
  entry .abs   absolute value: `S8`…`S64`, `F32`, `F64`
  entry .long   the value as a `Long`: `S8`…`S64`, and the unsigned value of `U8`…`U32`
  entry .int   the value as an `Int`: `S8`, `S16`, `S32`, and the unsigned value of `U8` and `U16`
  entry .short   the value as a `Short`: `S8`, `S16`, and the unsigned value of `U8`
  entry .byte   the raw byte of an `S8` or `U8`
  entry .octal   the value in base 8: every `U*` and `S*` type; padded to the width on `B8`…`B64`
  entry .hex   the value in base 16: every `U*` and `S*` type; padded to the width on `B8`…`B64`
  entry .base32   the value in base 32: every `U*` and `S*` type
  entry .binary   the value in base 2: every `U*` and `S*` type; padded to the width on `B8`…`B64`
  entry .text   the unsigned decimal value of a `U8`…`U64`, as `Text`
  entry .%%   floor-modulus on `S8`…`S64`
  entry ./-   floor-division on `S8`…`S64`
  entry .%   truncating remainder on `S8`…`S64`, and unsigned remainder on `U8`…`U64`
  entry .**   power, to a `Double` on `S8`…`S64`, and to the same type on `F32` and `F64`
  entry .bits   the bit pattern of any `U*`, `S*` or `F*` value as the `B*` of its width
  entry .gcd   greatest common divisor on every `U*` and `S*` type
  entry .s8   a `B8` reinterpreted as signed
  entry .u8   a `B8` reinterpreted as unsigned
  entry .s16   a `B16` reinterpreted as signed, or a `U8` widened
  entry .u16   a `B16` reinterpreted as unsigned, or a `U8` widened
  entry .s32   a `B32` reinterpreted as signed, or a `U8` or `U16` widened
  entry .u32   a `B32` reinterpreted as unsigned, or a `U8` or `U16` widened
  entry .s64   a `B64` reinterpreted as signed, or a `U8`, `U16` or `U32` widened
  entry .u64   a `B64` reinterpreted as unsigned, or a `U8`, `U16` or `U32` widened
  entry .apply   on a `B*`: the bits of an `Interval`, shifted down, or one bit by zero-based position
  entry .set   on a `B*`: the pattern with a bit, or a run of bits, set
  entry .clear   on a `B*`: the pattern with a bit, or a run of bits, cleared
  entry .flip   on a `B*`: the pattern with a bit, or a run of bits, inverted
  entry .bit   on a `B*`: whether the bit at an ordinal is set; `Prim` is the least significant
  entry .<<<   on a `B*`: rotation left by a count of bits
  entry .>>>   on a `B*`: rotation right by a count of bits
  entry .<<   on a `B*`: shift left
  entry .>>   on a `B*`: logical shift right, never sign-extending
  entry .&   on a `B*`: bitwise and, with any operand which converts to the pattern type
  entry .|   on a `B*`: bitwise or
  entry .^   on a `B*`: bitwise exclusive or
  entry .unary_~   on a `B*`: bitwise inversion, as prefix `~`
  entry .leadingZeros   on a `B*`: the count of zero bits above the highest set bit
  entry .trailingZeros   on a `B*`: the count of zero bits below the lowest set bit
  entry .ones   on a `B*`: the population count, as an `S32`
  entry .zeros   on a `B*`: the width less the population count, as an `S32`
  entry .reverse   on a `B*`: the pattern with its bits in reverse order
  entry .bytes   on a `B16`, `B32` or `B64`: the pattern as big-endian bytes
  entry .double   the `Double` of an `F64` or `F32`
  entry .float   the `Float` of an `F32`
  entry .mantissa   the low 52 bits of an `F64`'s pattern, as a `B64`
  entry .exponent   the unbiased exponent of an `F64` (as a `B16`) or an `F32` (as an `Int`)
  entry .sqrt   the square root of an `F64` or `F32`
  entry .cbrt   the cube root of an `F64` or `F32`
  entry .ceiling   the ceiling of an `F64` or `F32`
  entry .floor   the floor of an `F64` or `F32`
  entry .increment   the next `F64` or `F32` up
  entry .decrement   the next `F64` or `F32` down
  entry .successor   the next `F64` or `F32` up, as `increment`
  entry .predecessor   the next `F64` or `F32` down, as `decrement`
  entry .round   an `F64` or `F32` rounded to the nearest `Long`
  entry .scalb   an `F64` or `F32` multiplied by a power of two
  entry .signum   the sign of an `F64` or `F32`, as `-1.0 | 0.0 | 1.0`
  entry .ulp   the unit in the last place of an `F64` or `F32`
  entry .rawBits   the IEEE pattern of an `F64` or `F32`, `NaN` payloads preserved
  entry .finite   whether an `F64` or `F32` is finite
  entry .infinite   whether an `F64` or `F32` is infinite
  entry .nan   whether an `F64` or `F32` is `NaN`

entry .protointernal   the macro implementations behind `bin"…"`, `hex"…"`, the typed literals and chained comparison
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home.
  entry .bin   the macro behind `bin"…"`: validates the digits and splices a `Byte`, `Short`, `Int` or `Long`
  entry .hex   the macro behind `hex"…"`: validates the digits and splices a frozen byte array
  entry .parseU64   parses the digits of a `U64` literal at compile time, through `FromDigits`
  entry .parseS64   parses the digits of an `S64` literal
  entry .parseU32   parses the digits of a `U32` literal
  entry .parseS32   parses the digits of an `S32` literal
  entry .parseU16   parses the digits of a `U16` literal, halting if out of range
  entry .parseS16   parses the digits of an `S16` literal, halting if out of range
  entry .parseU8   parses the digits of a `U8` literal, halting if out of range
  entry .parseS8   parses the digits of an `S8` literal, halting if out of range
  entry .commensurable   the macro behind `Commensurable.numeric`: rewrites `(a < x) < b` into `a < x && x < b`

entry .Checkable   the typeclass behind `===` and `!==`: whether a value checks against another, possibly of another type
  detail
      A `left is Checkable against right` decides whether a `left` matches a `right`, and is
      what `===` consults. It is deliberately looser than `==`: the two sides may differ in
      type, so a value may be checked against a `Tolerance` (`x === 1.0 ± 0.01`), a frozen
      array against another by its contents, or a `Chain` against a `Chain` element by
      element, and a test assertion written with `===` reads as the comparison it means.
      It is the comparison probably's assertions use, and lives in `hypotenuse.check`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      3.0 === 3.0                 // true, through `Checkable.commensurable`
      3.01 === 3.0 ± 0.1          // true, through `Checkable.tolerance2`
      3.2 !== 3.0 +/- 0.1         // true

      given Text is Checkable against Text = Checkable(_.lower == _.lower)
      t"Hello" === t"hello"       // true
      ```

      An instance is written as a lambda, through `.apply`, and `#contramap` derives one for
      a type by mapping it into the subject.
  refer hypotenuse.Tolerance
  refer hypotenuse.===
  entry .frozenArray   two frozen arrays check equal when their elements do, whatever their element types
  entry .stream   two `Chain`s check equal element by element, when their elements are `Checkable`
      The lengths must agree: a shorter chain is not a prefix match.
  entry .tolerance2   a value checks against a `Tolerance` of its type when the tolerance covers it
  entry .commensurable   any type comparable with itself checks equal when neither is less than the other
      `left <= right && right <= left`, through the `Commensurable` in scope, so every numeric
      type is `Checkable` against itself with no instance of its own.
  entry .apply   `Checkable((left, right) => …)`: an instance from a predicate
  entry #check   whether `left` matches `right`
  entry #contramap   an instance for another type, by mapping it into this one before checking

entry .Tolerance   a value with a margin either side of it, which `===` matches a value against
  detail
      `Tolerance(base, tolerance)` covers every value from `base - tolerance` to
      `base + tolerance`, bounds included. One is made with `+/-` or `±` on any type with an `Orderable`, an
      `Addable` and a `Subtractable` by itself, and checked against with `===`:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val reading = 9.81
      reading === 9.8 ± 0.05     // true
      reading === 9.8 ± 0.01     // false
      (10 ± 2).covers(12)        // true: the bounds are included
      ```
  refer hypotenuse.Checkable
  refer hypotenuse.±
  entry #base   the centre of the range
  entry #tolerance   the half-width of the range
  entry #covers   whether a value lies within the range, bounds included

entry .===   `left === right`: whether `left` checks against `right`, through a `Checkable`
    An extension on any value, in `hypotenuse.check`, taking the `left is Checkable against
    right` in scope. Unlike `==`, the two sides may be of different types, so a number can
    be compared against a `Tolerance`, and a type's notion of equivalence for testing can be
    given without touching its `equals`.

entry .!==   `left !== right`: the negation of `===`

entry .+/-   `base +/- tolerance`: a `Tolerance` for `===` to match against
    An extension on any value whose type is `Commensurable` against itself, `Addable` by
    itself to itself and `Subtractable` by itself to itself: the primitives, the typed
    numbers, quantities. `10 +/- 2` covers every value from `8` to `12`, bounds included,
    through the `>=` of the type's `Commensurable`.

entry .±   `base ± tolerance`: the same `Tolerance` as `+/-`, for a keyboard with the symbol
